-\subsection*{Questions}
-\begin{enumerate}
-\item Is there a torsion-free finitely presented non-MF group?  For
-torsion-free groups, Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} gives no obstruction
-because every finite subgroup is trivial, while
-Theorem~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan} does apply.  What remains is
-group-theoretic: a torsion-free finitely presented group whose
-subgroup $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ contains a \emph{nontrivial} normal
-property-\textup{(T)} subgroup.
-Remark~\ref{rem:ff-realization} gives a small-cancellation construction over
-the torsion-free finitely presented Kazhdan group of \cite{FFF}, which
-removed the analogous torsion obstacle for nonsoficity.  The Clifford
-construction of Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-clifford} cannot produce such
-a group: it imposes $c^2=1$, so $\hat c^2=1$, and a torsion-free quotient
-$q$ then has $q(\hat c)=1$ and $q(N_{\mathrm{conj}})=1$.
-\leanverified{Sofic/TorsionFreeFiniteNormalLimit}{GroupApproximation.finiteSubgroup_eq_bot_of_isMulTorsionFree}
